The critical heat flux, CHF, is the limit at which the transition from nucleate boiling regime to film boiling occurs. The heat transfer mechanisms in film boiling are heat conduction and radiation through the vapor film. As heat transfer in film boiling is strongly degraded with respect to nucleate boiling, the knowledge of the CHF is important for the design of cooling systems. The CHF theory developed first by Kutateladze and then Zuber reports that the film boiling is triggered by hydrodynamic instabilities [8][9]. Close to the CHF, the vapor emanating from the hot surface forms jets evenly distributed over the surface. An influx of cold liquid descending towards the surface cools it and maintains a sufficiently cold surface. The velocity of the cold liquid is different from that of the rising column of vapor and a Kelvin/Helmholtz instability appears at the contact surface between the liquid and the vapor. This instability induces an oscillation of the vapor columns diameter which is based on the Helmholtz wavelength depending on the dissipated heat flux, gravity and physical parameters of the fluid. The critical value of this wavelength corresponds to the one allowing the vapor jets to merge and prevent the surface from being rewetted by the cold liquid, thus forming a vapor film on the surface. The CHF is then associated with the heat flow for which the wavelength reaches its critical value. The heat flux expression at which the vapor film appears is defined in Eq. (5). It depends on K an empirical constant, h l v the fluid latent heat, ρ l and ρ v the density of the liquid and vapor phase, Δ ρ the density difference between the liquid and vapor phase, σ the surface tension and g the Earth's gravity constant. Q C H F = K h l v ρ v ( σ g Δ ρ ρ v 2 ) 1 4 
 
To study the gravity dependence, different gravity compensation methods have been implemented with their advantages and drawbacks. CHF measurements for water at gravity levels equivalent from 5% to 0.1% of Earth's gravity have been made using parabolic flights [10]. Although the gravity dependence of the CHF is well highlighted by the measurements, the measured CHF are systematically lower than the theory. The authors assumed that thermocapillary effects become the preferential heat transport mechanism at very low gravity and induce a difference with the hydrodynamic instability theory. Similar measurements have also been conducted in free fall tower which allow reaching levels of gravity equivalent to those in parabolic flights but for shorter experiments [11]. They showed that the critical heat flux of CFC-113 for a gravity equivalent to 0.01% of the Earth's gravity is divided by 5 compared to normal gravity. However, the CHF reduction is lower than predicted by the theory. Kim and coworkers investigated the influence of gravity and subcooling on pool boiling heat transfers for FC-72 [12]. Using parabolic maneuver with a KC-135 airplane they successfully reached gravity level ranging from few percent of Earth's gravity up to 1.7g. They demonstrated the CHF enhancement at high-g and reduction at low-g. However, as expected from the g 1 4 dependence, the CHF variation is less noticeable in hypergravity than in low-g. Raj et al. compared experimental CHF data obtained during parabolic flight for FC-72 in a gravity range from 0g to 1.7g [13]. They obtained similar results to Kim et al., but showed that CHF can be successfully predicted at high-g using gravity as a scaling parameter; the prediction being valid when the boiling regime is buoyancy-dominated (gravity > 0.1g). These gravity compensation methods allow reaching very low levels of resulting gravity (up to 0.01% of Earth's gravity) but do not allow long experiment duration due to limited time in free fall tower or parabolic flight. The resulting gravity is also very difficult to adjust with these methods leading to a lack of experimental data between 0.1g and 1g. 
 
For the study of dia- or para-magnetic fluids, the magnetic forces enable total or partial compensation of gravity. Magnetic levitation allows the study of fluids in low-g and high-g environments but also in weightlessness conditions [14]. It allows a fine adjustment of the resulting gravity without any limitation on the experiment duration, it is thus very suitable for the study of heat transfer particularly in steady-state conditions. However, the limit of this compensation method is the fluid sensitivity to magnetic forces, the greater the magnetic susceptibility the easier the fluid is to levitate. Pichavant characterized oxygen CHF near the critical point in low-g environment using a superconducting solenoid [15]. Measurements at very low gravity levels highlight the CHF gravity dependence. However, the absence of measurements at intermediate levels between 0.01g and 1g makes it impossible to correlate the results with the CHF theory. Quettier et al. studied the critical flux in liquid helium down to 0.5g using a 
superconducting solenoid [16]. They have successfully shown the g 1 4 CHF dependency in the studied gravity range and demonstrated the viability of this technique. However the high gravity field inhomogeneity induced by the magnet geometry could have influenced the results as the gravity was not homogeneous on the heat exchange surface used for CHF characterization, this effect was not considered during the results analysis contrary to the present work. 
 
The lack of liquid helium CHF measurements at gravity levels <0.5g and >1g is problematic for the design of future high magnetic field HTS magnets. The knowledge of CHF for a very wide range of gravity, 0g-2g, will allow for an improvement of the magnet cooling systems and a possible extension of the magnet operating times. This study will focus on the gravity influence on the liquid helium CHF using a 
resistive magnet allowing to obtain gravity levels between 0g and 2.2g. 
 
